e('If an attacker changes even one bit of the message, they can\'t compute the matching tag without the key. So a valid tag proves two things: the message hasn\'t been altered (**integrity**), and it was produced by someone with the key (**authenticity**).','With a suitable MAC, protected key and correct verification, forging a tag for a new message is computationally infeasible within the intended security bounds. A verified tag supports integrity and key-holder authenticity; tag guessing has a nonzero probability, and replay does not establish freshness.','Cryptographicsecuritynotabsoluteproof.')
e('A receiver can\'t prove to a judge that the *sender* wrote it. For that you need a digital signature, made with a private key only one party holds.','The tag alone cannot distinguish which shared-key holder created it. Digital signatures separate signing from verification authority, but attribution to a human and legal effect require additional evidence.','Noautomaticlegalassurance.')
e('A captured `(message, tag)` pair stays valid forever.','A captured `(message, tag)` pair remains cryptographically valid while that key and message interpretation are accepted.','Keyrotation revocation scope.')
e('Many parsers take the last `amount`, so the forged request goes through.','Application abuse also requires the server to accept the inserted padding and interpret the extra parameter as intended; parser and encoding behaviour matter.','ValidMACnotautomaticsemanticexploit.')
e("K'   = key padded to the block size","K'   = hash key first if over block size,\n       then zero-pad to block size",'Diagramomitslongkeynormalisation.')
e('- The tag can be truncated (HMAC-SHA256-128 keeps 16 bytes), trading tag length for forgery resistance. Don\'t go below about 64 bits.','- Tags can be truncated: 128 bits retains 16 bytes from HMAC-SHA256. Choose the length from the protocol, forgery budget and verification limits; 64 bits is not a universal safe minimum.','Taglimitsprotocoldependent.')
e('- Hashes without length extension, such as SHA-3 and BLAKE2, can be keyed directly. BLAKE2 has a built-in `key` parameter, and KMAC is the SHA-3 keyed mode.','- Use a specified keyed construction. BLAKE2 has a built-in key parameter, and KMAC is standardised in NIST SP 800-185. Absence of length extension alone does not prove an arbitrary keyed hash construction is a secure MAC.','Noextensionnotsecurityproof.',['https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7693','https://csrc.nist.gov/pubs/sp/800/185/final'])
e('Here is a complete webhook-style check in Python\'s standard library:','This example checks a lowercase hexadecimal body tag. It omits HTTP header handling, key provisioning/rotation and replay protection, so it is not a complete webhook receiver:','Explicitincompleteprotocol.')
e('import hmac, hashlib\n\nKEY = b"32-random-bytes-from-a-vault..."','import hmac, hashlib, secrets\n\n# Demo only: share and persist securely.\nKEY = secrets.token_bytes(32)','Literalwas29bytes notrandom.')
e('    good = sign(body)\n    return hmac.compare_digest(good, tag)','    if not isinstance(tag, str):\n        return False\n    if len(tag) != 64 or any(\n        c not in "0123456789abcdef"\n        for c in tag\n    ):\n        return False\n    good = sign(body)\n    return hmac.compare_digest(good, tag)','Rejectmalformed/nonASCII TypeError andwronglength.',[py])
e('The critical line is `hmac.compare_digest`. A normal `good == tag` comparison returns as soon as it finds the first differing character. That makes the comparison slightly faster when the first byte is wrong than when the first ten bytes are right.','Use `hmac.compare_digest` for fixed-type, fixed-length tag comparisons. Ordinary equality is not a constant-time API; implementations can compare machine-sized chunks or use other optimisations, so a byte-by-byte timing model is not guaranteed.','Runtimecomparisonnotguaranteedbyteoracle.',[py])
e('An attacker who can measure response times can exploit this **timing side channel**: try all 16 values of the first hex character, keep the one that takes longest, move to the next. For a 64-character hex tag that turns a 2^256 guessing problem into roughly 64 × 16 = 1,024 guesses, each repeated enough times to average out noise. Network jitter makes it harder, not impossible, especially with many repeated measurements. `compare_digest` takes the same time regardless of where the strings differ.','In an idealised oracle that reveals each additional matching character, testing 16 candidates at each of 64 positions uses about 1,024 candidate choices, plus repeated measurements. Real exploitability depends on implementation, noise and other checks; this is not a measured network attack. `compare_digest` avoids content-dependent early exit, but does not make the entire request path constant-time.','Separatetoyoraclefromguaranteedattack.',[py])
e('**AWS Signature Version 4.** Every AWS API request is signed with HMAC-SHA256.','**AWS Signature Version 4.** SigV4-authenticated requests use HMAC-SHA256; AWS also supports other authentication paths, including asymmetric SigV4a.','NotallAWSAPIHMAC.',[aws])
e('These are fast **one-time MACs** keyed freshly for each nonce, which is one reason nonce reuse is so damaging.','ChaCha20-Poly1305 derives a one-time Poly1305 key for each nonce. GCM reuses a GHASH subkey derived from the AES key and combines its result with a nonce-dependent mask; GHASH alone is not a complete secure MAC. Both modes require their specified nonce discipline.','GHASHkeynotfreshpernonce.',[g,ch])
e('| Encrypt-then-MAC | IPsec ESP | Secure |','| Encrypt-then-MAC | Traditional encryption-plus-integrity IPsec ESP suites | Sound under suitable primitive, key and encoding assumptions |','Notcompositionorderaloneproof.')
e('| MAC-then-encrypt | TLS 1.0–1.2 CBC | Fragile |','| MAC-then-encrypt | Original TLS CBC suites | Difficult to implement without side channels |','TLSCBCcanEtMextension.')
e('use an AEAD such as AES-GCM, which does encrypt-then-MAC correctly inside one vetted primitive.','use a maintained AEAD implementation such as AES-GCM or ChaCha20-Poly1305, following its nonce, tag and key-usage limits.','AEADnotgenericCBCMACinternals.',[g,ch])
e('- A MAC is a keyed checksum: a valid tag proves integrity and that a key holder produced the message.','- A suitable MAC supports integrity and key-holder authenticity under its security assumptions; it does not establish freshness or a particular human sender.','Consistencyprobabilisticsecurity.')
c(1,'prompt','Assume a protected random key and correctly implemented HMAC-SHA256 verification. A message tag matches. What security property does this support?','Avoidabsoluteproof.')
c(1,'explanation','Correct under the stated cryptographic assumptions. The tag supports authenticity to a key holder and integrity of the covered bytes; it is not an absolute guarantee against forgery or replay.','Finiteforgeryprobability.',option=1)
c(2,'prompt','A bank and customer app share an HMAC key. Can the valid tag alone distinguish whether the app or the bank generated a payment instruction?','Removelegalcourtquestion.')
c(2,'text','No: both hold the same key and can generate valid tags','Sharedkeyattribution.',option=3)
c(2,'explanation','Correct. A signature can separate signing authority from verification authority, but does not by itself prove which human acted or determine a legal outcome.','Noautomaticlegalproof.',option=3)
c(2,'workedExample','Both app and bank can compute the same valid tag, so it cannot distinguish them. With an asymmetric signature, verifiers can check without gaining signing authority. Trust in key binding, key custody and other evidence is still needed for attribution. Legal conclusions are outside this lesson.','Explicitlegalgap.')
c(3,'explanation','Correct for valid same-type tags: this API avoids content-dependent short-circuit comparison. Other request processing and type/length differences can still affect timing.','Scopeconstanttime.',[py],1)
c(3,'workedExample','Ordinary equality has no constant-time guarantee. compare_digest avoids content-dependent short-circuiting for appropriate inputs; validate the tag format and length, then compare consistently. This is not proof that the complete login or webhook endpoint has no timing leakage.','No byteoracleuniversal.',[py])
